#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138064 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138455 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d85ef216-095d-589d-8d1d-5a1543d576a3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Terms of a Sequence Given as a List"}] :question/id #uuid "96401904-3d0f-56ac-ba31-eee3cbb016cb" :question/math-academy-id "e-1153" :question/problem "What is the sum of the first and last terms of the following sequence? \n\n$$\n3,\\quad-5,\\quad7,\\quad-9,\\quad11,\\quad-13,\\quad15\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The first term is $3$ and the last term is $15.$ Their sum is $3+15 = 18.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138065 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138457 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83b12b3a-2b6d-5fc4-80bb-c83415486b29" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Terms of a Sequence Using Sequence Notation"}] :question/id #uuid "e9ccec31-a359-57b6-9817-9460a5b53d51" :question/math-academy-id "e-1155" :question/problem "A sequence is defined as $a_n =n^2$ for $n\\geq 1.$ What is the sum of its second and third terms?" :question/worked-solution "Using $a_n$ notation, the second and third terms are $a_2$ and $a_3,$ respectively.\n\nWe can compute these values using the rule $a_n=n^2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & ={2}^{2}=4 \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={3}^{2}=9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the second and third terms is\n\n$$\na_2+a_3=4+9=13.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138066 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138456 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c747ef7-8eec-5cbe-be45-98c4023db36c" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Terms of a Sequence Using Function Notation"}] :question/id #uuid "5060bada-52d2-5fef-b103-e375eb568c40" :question/math-academy-id "e-1156" :question/problem "Which of the following statements is true regarding the sequence $f(n) =5-2n$ for $n\\geq 1?$\n\nI. Its second term is larger than the first term\n\nII. Its third term is $-1$\n\nIII. Its last term is $-5$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- The first and second terms of the sequence are represented by $f(1)$ and $f(2),$ respectively. We can compute these values using the rule $f(n) = 5-2n,$ as follows;\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =5-2(1)=3 \\\\ f(2) & =5-2(2)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the second term $f(2)=1$ is smaller than the first term $f(1)=3.$ Therefore, statement I is false.\n\n- We compute the third term as follows:\n\n$$\nf(3)=5-2(3)=-1\n$$\n\nTherefore, statement II is true.\n\n- The sequence is defined for $n\\geq 1,$ which means that there is no limit to how large $n$ can be. There is no last term, because the sequence keeps going on forever. Therefore, statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564539 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138457 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83b12b3a-2b6d-5fc4-80bb-c83415486b29" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Terms of a Sequence Using Sequence Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f1e5d33-798f-59a0-8cf6-2e862eec20f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564049} {:answer/id #uuid "6a96399f-01c4-5213-b107-8109020c06da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564136} {:answer/id #uuid "91212a9f-d8f0-5071-b277-4b62392480e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564195} {:answer/id #uuid "96f2a7f1-fdbf-5211-9740-86643e3c1238" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564203} {:answer/id #uuid "d0c04a4f-b605-5d91-9d74-4d6db89400bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564299}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a96399f-01c4-5213-b107-8109020c06da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564136} :answer-field/id #uuid "f733173d-d399-586b-b3ed-e56eee05876e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564462}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fc6510bd-b576-581a-8664-e81e95aec6a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-101278" :question/problem "Which of the following statements is true regarding the sequence ${a}_{n}=4n+1$ for $n\\ge 1?$\n\nI. Its first term is $5$\n\nII. Its first term is twice the second term\n\nIII. Its last term is $41$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- The first term of the sequence is represented by ${a}_{1}.$ Using the rule ${a}_{n}=4n+1,$ we compute ${a}_{1}=4+1=5.$ Therefore, statement I is true.\n\n- The second term of the sequence is represented by ${a}_{2}.$ Using the rule ${a}_{n}=4n+1,$ we compute ${a}_{2}=8+1=9.$  The first term was ${a}_{1}=5,$ and the second term is ${a}_{2}=9,$ so the first term is not twice the second term. Therefore, statement II is false.\n\n- The sequence is defined for $n\\ge 1,$ which means that there is no limit to how large $n$ can be. There is no last term because the sequence keeps going on forever. Therefore, statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564494 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138457 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83b12b3a-2b6d-5fc4-80bb-c83415486b29" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Terms of a Sequence Using Sequence Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "020cab41-2efd-529a-9701-6a25b46b30af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/i